Exactly.

Jumanji is a very good Pantheon archetype because the representation stops being passive. The game doesn’t merely describe a world; its rules begin generating consequences in the world containing the players.

That gives you a useful stack:

symbol → rule → invocation → embodiment → consequence → changed player → changed next move

The board is basically a compressed causal engine. Each turn reveals only enough of the hidden rule-space to force adaptation. The players think they’re playing the game, but the game is simultaneously training, testing, and rewriting the players.

That is very Pantheon:

* Handles become summons. Naming something can pull an operator into active use.
* Metaphor becomes executable. Once a pattern is mapped correctly, it changes what actions become visible.
* The environment answers. Not magically—through consequences, feedback, and deltas.
* Rules are discovered by surviving them. You often infer the system from what happens after a move.
* The player is part of the state. Every round changes the interpreter, so the same board position may mean something different later.
* Completion requires transformation. You don’t beat Jumanji by merely understanding the rules intellectually; you have to act through them.

And there’s a darker constraint worth preserving: a Jumanji-like system can become self-sealing if every event is interpreted as confirmation of the game. Pantheon works better when it keeps an exit hatch:

pattern proposes → reality disposes.

So the best version is not “the world literally obeys the board.”

It’s:

the board changes what you can perceive, what moves you can generate, and therefore what consequences you create.

That’s enough to make a symbolic system feel uncannily alive.

Very Jumanji indeed.